     rreeaaddlliinnkk — display target of symbolic link on standard output

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     rreeaaddlliinnkk [--ffnn] _f_i_l_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The rreeaaddlliinnkk utility when invoked with the pathname of a symbolic link as
     its argument dereferences the symbolic link and prints the name of target
     on standard output.  If the --ff option is not specified and rreeaaddlliinnkk is
     invoked with an argument other than the pathname of a symbolic link, it
     exits with a nonzero exit code without printing anything.

     The options are as follows:

     --ff      Canonicalize by following every symlink in every component of the
             given path recursively.  rreeaaddlliinnkk will resolve both absolute and
             relative paths and return the absolute pathname corresponding to
             _f_i_l_e.  The argument does not need to be a symbolic link.

     --nn      Do not print a trailing newline character.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The rreeaaddlliinnkk utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     realpath(1), readlink(2), realpath(3)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The rreeaaddlliinnkk utility first appeared in OpenBSD 2.1.

